#version 460
// PHOTON R5 — RAY-TRACED REFLECTIONS. Screen first, rays for the rest.
//
// WHAT THIS SHADER IS FOR, in one sentence: the screen-space march already
// answers most reflective pixels exactly (it reflects SHADED pixels, which is
// the sharpest and cheapest source there is), and this shader answers only the
// ones it could not — off screen, behind an occluder, past the edge fade, or in
// the roughness band the march declines. That ordering is Lumen's and it is the
// right one here for a measurable reason: a ray's hit is shaded from the
// Photon voxels, whose finest directional mip is HALF the voxel grid, while a
// screen hit is a real pixel of this scene. Where the screen has an answer it
// is the better answer.
//
// WHERE THE ANSWER GOES. Straight into `jahSsrReflection`, the RGBA16F texture
// HlmsPbs samples as `ssrTexture` and composites with ogre-patch 0036's single
// lerp:
//     envColourS = lerp( envColourS, ssrReflection.rgb, ssrReflection.w )
// so `w` is literally "how much of the probe/sky answer does the trace
// replace". NEVER an add: the same lobe twice, through a history loop, is what
// patch 0036 exists to stop (PHOTON_SPEC §11 rule 3). Where this shader writes
// nothing, the texture still holds exactly what JahSsrResolve wrote, which is
// exactly today's picture.
//
// THE SCREEN'S SHARE IS KEPT, NOT OVERWRITTEN. A pixel whose march came back
// with a PARTIAL weight (the resolve's envelope, its roughness ramp, its hit
// mask's antialiased edge) is not a pixel with no answer — it is a pixel with
// a fraction of one. The composite is linear in that fraction, so this shader
// writes `rgb = mix( traced, screen, screenW )` with `w` = its own gate: the
// screen keeps its share, the ray fills the remainder, and the sum is one
// answer at one weight rather than two images fighting (lane SSR-2's rule,
// arrived at from the other side).
//
// ONE RAY PER PIXEL PER FRAME, and a temporal mean behind it. The lobe is
// sampled with the GGX VNDF (Heitz 2018), which is the distribution of visible
// normals — the estimator whose weight is 1 for the whole single-scatter GGX
// specular BRDF, so no per-sample weight appears anywhere below and a mirror
// (alpha -> 0) collapses to the exact mirror direction with zero variance. One
// sample of a glossy lobe is noise; the accumulator in `history` is the
// integral, reprojected through the previous frame's view-projection and
// validated against the previous frame's distance (see `historyAt`).
//
// COMPILED AT BUILD TIME by glslangValidator at --target-env spirv1.4 (what
// SPV_KHR_ray_query documents), like rq_rays.comp beside it: Ogre's own GLSL
// path never sets a target environment, so this never goes through it.
#extension GL_EXT_ray_query : require
#extension GL_GOOGLE_include_directive : require
#extension GL_EXT_buffer_reference : require
#extension GL_EXT_buffer_reference_uvec2 : require

layout( local_size_x = 8, local_size_y = 8, local_size_z = 1 ) in;

/// HOW MANY CASCADES THE HIT-SHADING READS. The chain is at most this long in
/// the engine's tier table; the C++ side binds the innermost `count` and
/// repeats the last one into the unused slots (a descriptor array may not have
/// holes).
const int kMaxCascades = 4;

layout( set = 0, binding = 0 ) uniform accelerationStructureEXT tlas;

layout( set = 0, binding = 1 ) uniform Params
{
	/// xyz = the camera in world space, w = 1 for a perspective camera and 0
	/// for an orthographic one (the reconstruction differs — see worldAt()).
	vec4 camPos;
	/// The world-space ray basis: worldDir(u,v) = rayTL + rayRight*u + rayDown*v,
	/// scaled so its component along the camera's forward axis is exactly 1.
	/// Then `camPos + worldDir * viewDistance` is the surface. Under an
	/// orthographic camera rayTL/rayRight/rayDown carry the image PLANE instead
	/// (an offset, not a ray) and `fwd` is the one view direction.
	vec4 rayTL;
	vec4 rayRight;
	vec4 rayDown;
	vec4 fwd;
	/// x = A, y = B of Camera::getProjectionParamsAB() (reverse-Z safe),
	/// z = the far plane (the orthographic depth scale), w = the roughness
	/// gate's FEATHER half-width (kRayReflectFeather).
	vec4 projParams;
	/// xy = the trace resolution in pixels, zw = the full-resolution size of
	/// jahSsrReflection.
	vec4 resolution;
	/// x = the roughness gate — the PROJECT's dial, not a renderer constant:
	/// above it the probe/field photograph is a good enough integral of a wide
	/// lobe and a ray is spent for nothing. The transition is FEATHERED over
	/// projParams.w either side of it. y = the ray's maximum length in
	/// world units, z = the frame index (the sample sequence's only input),
	/// w = how many cascades are bound.
	vec4 knobs;
	/// x = 1 when the voxel textures carry the anisotropic slots, y = the
	/// surface bias in world units (a ray starts this far off its own surface),
	/// z = 1 when a sky cubemap is bound, w = the history's minimum blend
	/// weight (the floor on 1/n — how long the temporal mean remembers).
	vec4 knobs2;
	/// The colour an escaping ray reads when no sky cubemap is bound.
	vec4 skyColour;
	/// The VIEW space's three axes in world space (the camera's orientation as
	/// a rotation): what turns the G-buffer's view-space normal back into one.
	vec4 viewAxisX;
	vec4 viewAxisY;
	vec4 viewAxisZ;
	/// Per cascade: xyz = the voxel box's world origin, w = the box's largest
	/// side in world units (the cell size times the resolution).
	vec4 voxelOrigin[kMaxCascades];
	/// Per cascade: xyz = 1 / the voxel box's world size, w = one cell in world
	/// units (the sample point's bias along the ray).
	vec4 voxelInvSize[kMaxCascades];
	/// THE PREVIOUS FRAME'S CAMERA, in exactly the same five numbers as this
	/// frame's (camPos, rayTL, rayRight, rayDown, fwd). Not a view-projection
	/// matrix, deliberately: inverting the basis above is the same arithmetic
	/// forwards and backwards, so the reprojection cannot disagree with the
	/// reconstruction about a handedness, a y-flip or a depth convention — and
	/// each of those is a silent half-pixel-to-whole-image error that only shows
	/// up as a smear while the camera moves.
	vec4 prevCamPos;
	vec4 prevRayTL;
	vec4 prevRayRight;
	vec4 prevRayDown;
	vec4 prevFwd;
	/// ---- THE SECOND EYE (lane REFLECT-VR-1) -----------------------------
	/// x = 1 when this target carries TWO EYES SIDE BY SIDE — the left eye in
	/// the left half of every texture here, the right in the right half
	/// (chain::applyStereo's viewport split). Then the five basis vectors above
	/// are the LEFT eye's and the five below are the right eye's, each mapped
	/// over its OWN half as a 0..1 image; with x = 0 the block below is unread
	/// and the basis above is the one camera's, exactly as before.
	///
	/// WHY A HALF TEST AND NOT TWO DISPATCHES: the depth, the normals and the
	/// roughness of BOTH eyes live in one texture at one scale, and the pixel's
	/// own coordinate already selects the right eye's texels. Only the RAY
	/// needs to know which eye it belongs to — one branch on the x coordinate,
	/// no second descriptor set, no second history ring, and the seam falls on
	/// a block boundary by construction (the C++ side sizes the trace so).
	///
	/// y = THE VIEW'S AGE: how many consecutive frames this history has been
	/// written (0 on a first frame, a resize, a scene bind). See `historyAt`.
	vec4 stereo;
	vec4 camPos2;
	vec4 rayTL2;
	vec4 rayRight2;
	vec4 rayDown2;
	vec4 fwd2;
	vec4 prevCamPos2;
	vec4 prevRayTL2;
	vec4 prevRayRight2;
	vec4 prevRayDown2;
	vec4 prevFwd2;
	/// ---- THE SURFACE CACHE (PHOTON-CARDS-2) ------------------------------
	/// x = the instance-table entries bound (0 = the scene holds no cache: the
	/// card read declines every hit and the voxels answer, as before), y = the
	/// card records bound, z = the footprint gate in card texels
	/// (Types.h kCardFootprintTexels), w = the per-slot geometry-row entries
	/// bound (the hit's geometric normal; 0 = none — the reversed ray faces).
	vec4 cards;
} p;

/// ONE EYE'S IMAGE, as the five numbers everything below needs: where it is,
/// and the world-space ray (or image-plane offset) of its own 0..1 image.
/// @see p.stereo — with one eye there is one of these and nothing branches.
struct EyeImage
{
	vec3  camPos;
	float perspective;		// 1 = a ray per pixel, 0 = an offset (orthographic)
	vec3  rayTL;
	vec3  rayRight;
	vec3  rayDown;
	vec3  fwd;
};

EyeImage eyeNow( int eye )
{
	EyeImage e;
	if( eye == 1 )
	{
		e.camPos = p.camPos2.xyz; e.perspective = p.camPos2.w;
		e.rayTL = p.rayTL2.xyz; e.rayRight = p.rayRight2.xyz;
		e.rayDown = p.rayDown2.xyz; e.fwd = p.fwd2.xyz;
	}
	else
	{
		e.camPos = p.camPos.xyz; e.perspective = p.camPos.w;
		e.rayTL = p.rayTL.xyz; e.rayRight = p.rayRight.xyz;
		e.rayDown = p.rayDown.xyz; e.fwd = p.fwd.xyz;
	}
	return e;
}

EyeImage eyeBefore( int eye )
{
	EyeImage e;
	if( eye == 1 )
	{
		e.camPos = p.prevCamPos2.xyz; e.perspective = p.prevCamPos2.w;
		e.rayTL = p.prevRayTL2.xyz; e.rayRight = p.prevRayRight2.xyz;
		e.rayDown = p.prevRayDown2.xyz; e.fwd = p.prevFwd2.xyz;
	}
	else
	{
		e.camPos = p.prevCamPos.xyz; e.perspective = p.prevCamPos.w;
		e.rayTL = p.prevRayTL.xyz; e.rayRight = p.prevRayRight.xyz;
		e.rayDown = p.prevRayDown.xyz; e.fwd = p.prevFwd.xyz;
	}
	return e;
}

/// WHICH EYE OWNS A COLUMN, and the whole of the stereo layout in one place:
/// the left eye is the left half of the image, the right eye the right half.
int eyeOfColumn( int x, int width )
{
	return ( p.stereo.x > 0.5 && x >= width / 2 ) ? 1 : 0;
}
/// That eye's own 0..1 image coordinate from a coordinate over the whole
/// target. Horizontally it is the half's own fraction; vertically nothing is
/// split, so it passes straight through.
vec2 eyeLocalUv( vec2 uv, int eye )
{
	if( p.stereo.x <= 0.5 )
		return uv;
	return vec2( clamp( uv.x * 2.0 - float( eye ), 0.0, 1.0 ), uv.y );
}
/// ...and back: a coordinate in the eye's own image, as one over the target.
vec2 eyeGlobalUv( vec2 local, int eye )
{
	if( p.stereo.x <= 0.5 )
		return local;
	return vec2( ( local.x + float( eye ) ) * 0.5, local.y );
}

layout( set = 0, binding = 2 ) uniform sampler2D gBufNormals;
layout( set = 0, binding = 3 ) uniform sampler2D gBufShadowRoughness;
layout( set = 0, binding = 4 ) uniform sampler2D sceneDepth;
layout( rgba16f, set = 0, binding = 5 ) uniform image2D ssrReflection;
/// THE TEMPORAL MEAN, PING-PONGED. Two buffers and not one, because a pixel
/// reads the mean at its own REPROJECTED coordinate — any pixel of the buffer —
/// while its neighbours are writing theirs. One buffer makes that a read/write
/// race within a single dispatch (the value read would be this frame's or last
/// frame's depending on the scheduler), and a temporal filter fed by a race is
/// how a stable picture acquires a shimmer nobody can reproduce. The pair costs
/// one extra image of 8 bytes per traced pixel.
layout( rgba16f, set = 0, binding = 6 ) uniform readonly  image2D historyPrev;
layout( rgba16f, set = 0, binding = 7 ) uniform image2D historyCur;
/// ...and the distance beside it, which is what makes the reprojection a TEST
/// rather than a hope: the surface found at the reprojected pixel is the same
/// surface only if it was the same distance away.
layout( rg32f, set = 0, binding = 8 ) uniform readonly  image2D historyDistPrev;
layout( rg32f, set = 0, binding = 9 ) uniform image2D historyDistCur;
layout( set = 0, binding = 10 ) uniform sampler3D voxelIso[kMaxCascades];
layout( set = 0, binding = 11 ) uniform sampler3D voxelX[kMaxCascades];
layout( set = 0, binding = 12 ) uniform sampler3D voxelY[kMaxCascades];
layout( set = 0, binding = 13 ) uniform sampler3D voxelZ[kMaxCascades];
layout( set = 0, binding = 14 ) uniform samplerCube skyCube;

/// The same ceiling JahSsrResolve_ps.glsl and JahSsrHistory_ps.glsl hold the
/// SSR loop to, and for the same reason: about twelve stops above white, six
/// below the half-float format's own ceiling. A reflection clamped here is
/// still white after any exposure the tonemapper arrives at.
/// ...and it lives in the shared hit header now, with the two NaN predicates
/// and the voxel/sky functions this file used to own outright (GATHER-1a):
/// `kMaxRadiance`, `finite1`, `finite3`, `jahVoxelRadiance`, `jahSkyRadiance`.
/// HOW MANY SAMPLES THE FILTER'S CONFIDENCE RAMP WANTS before it composites a
/// traced reflection at full weight. Declared in BOTH stages because the two
/// are separate GLSL translation units sharing one descriptor set, and the
/// seeding rule in main() has to state the same number the filter reads —
/// rq_reflect_filter.comp's kWarmupFrames. Change one, change the other.
const float kWarmupFrames = 8.0;

/// THE SHARED HIT HEADER — `kMaxRadiance`, the two NaN predicates (`x == x` is
/// not a NaN test in this tree: CLAUDE.md, HDR-1), `jahVoxelRadiance` and
/// `jahSkyRadiance`. It reads this file's own bindings through the macros
/// below, so nothing about the descriptor set moved when the functions did
/// (GATHER-1a; the gather traces the same cache and two consumers of one cache
/// that can disagree about what a voxel MEANS is a defect waiting to happen).
#define JAH_VOX_COUNT       int( p.knobs.w )
#define JAH_VOX_ANISO       ( p.knobs2.x > 0.5 )
#define JAH_VOX_ORIGIN( c )  p.voxelOrigin[c]
#define JAH_VOX_INVSIZE( c ) p.voxelInvSize[c]
#define JAH_SKY_ON          ( p.knobs2.z > 0.5 )
#define JAH_SKY_COLOUR      p.skyColour.xyz
#include "jah_rq_hit.glsl"

/// THE SURFACE CACHE the hits read FIRST (PHOTON-CARDS-2, SC-1d): the card
/// table, the instance table, the Depth and Radiance layers, at bindings 15-18.
#define JAH_CARD_BINDING_BASE 15
#include "jah_rq_card_bindings.glsl"
#define JAH_CARD_SLOTS   uint( p.cards.x )
#define JAH_CARD_RECORDS uint( p.cards.y )
#define JAH_CARD_FOOTPRINT_TEXELS p.cards.z
#include "jah_rq_card.glsl"

/// THE HIT'S GEOMETRIC NORMAL (the card pick's facing): the per-slot row table
/// the tier writes with the TLAS (binding 19) and the GPU scene's geometry rows
/// (binding 20, three uvec4 lanes a row).
layout( std430, set = 0, binding = 19 ) readonly buffer JahGeomRowOfSlot { uint jahGeomRowOfSlot[]; };
layout( std430, set = 0, binding = 20 ) readonly buffer JahGeomRows { uvec4 jahGeomRows[]; };
#define JAH_GEOM_SLOTS uint( p.cards.w )
#define JAH_GEOM_ROW_OF( slot ) jahGeomRowOfSlot[slot]
#define JAH_GEOM_LANE( row, k ) jahGeomRows[( row ) * 3u + ( k )]
#include "jah_rq_geom.glsl"

/// Metres along the camera's forward axis, with and without reverse Z — the
/// same line JahSsrRayMarch_ps.glsl uses, from Camera::getProjectionParamsAB().
float viewDistance( float rawDepth )
{
	if( p.camPos.w < 0.5 )				// orthographic: depth is linear in distance
		return abs( ( rawDepth - p.projParams.x ) / p.projParams.y );
	return p.projParams.y / ( rawDepth - p.projParams.x );
}

/// The surface this pixel shows, in world space.
/// THE SURFACE THIS PIXEL DREW, from its depth. `uv` is the coordinate in the
/// EYE'S OWN image (eyeLocalUv), so under stereo each half reconstructs through
/// the eye that rendered it and nothing here knows there are two.
vec3 worldAt( EyeImage e, vec2 uv, float dist )
{
	const vec3 plane = e.rayTL + e.rayRight * uv.x + e.rayDown * uv.y;
	if( e.perspective < 0.5 )
		return e.camPos + plane + e.fwd * dist;		// an offset, not a ray
	return e.camPos + plane * dist;
}

/// A cheap, well-distributed per-pixel-per-frame pair. PCG's mixing step; the
/// sequence's ONLY input is the pixel and the frame, so a still camera on a
/// still scene still averages a different ray every frame, which is what makes
/// the temporal mean an integral rather than a repeat of one sample.
uint pcg( uint v )
{
	uint state = v * 747796405u + 2891336453u;
	uint word = ( ( state >> ( ( state >> 28u ) + 4u ) ) ^ state ) * 277803737u;
	return ( word >> 22u ) ^ word;
}
vec2 sample2( ivec2 pix, uint frame )
{
	const uint h = pcg( uint( pix.x ) * 1973u + uint( pix.y ) * 9277u + frame * 26699u );
	return vec2( float( h & 0xFFFFu ) / 65536.0, float( h >> 16u ) / 65536.0 );
}

/// THE GGX VISIBLE-NORMAL SAMPLE (Heitz, "Sampling the GGX Distribution of
/// Visible Normals", JCGT 2018). Returns a half vector in the surface's
/// tangent frame given the view direction in that frame.
///
/// WHAT IS DROPPED, stated because "the weight is 1" is the half-truth this
/// file used to tell. Sampling the VNDF cancels D and the masking term, leaving
/// the estimator weight `F * G2 / G1` — the Fresnel term and the height-
/// correlated shadowing-to-masking ratio. NEITHER is applied here, and that is
/// the design rather than an omission: what this pass writes is a RADIANCE plus
/// a confidence, and HlmsPbs multiplies it by the surface's own BRDF when it
/// composites `envColourS` — applying F here would apply it twice. G2/G1 is
/// dropped outright: it is 1 at normal incidence and falls off at grazing
/// angles, so a grazing reflection is slightly too bright, which is the same
/// approximation the probe path this replaces makes. Correcting it needs the
/// hit's own normal, which the acceleration structure does not hand back (see
/// jah_rq_card.glsl on VK_KHR_ray_tracing_position_fetch).
///
/// At alpha 0 it returns the surface normal exactly, which is why a mirror
/// traces the mirror direction with no noise at all.
vec3 sampleGgxVndf( vec3 Ve, float alpha, vec2 u )
{
	const vec3 Vh = normalize( vec3( alpha * Ve.x, alpha * Ve.y, Ve.z ) );
	const float lensq = Vh.x * Vh.x + Vh.y * Vh.y;
	const vec3 T1 = lensq > 0.0 ? vec3( -Vh.y, Vh.x, 0.0 ) * inversesqrt( lensq )
								: vec3( 1.0, 0.0, 0.0 );
	const vec3 T2 = cross( Vh, T1 );
	const float r = sqrt( u.x );
	const float phi = 6.2831853 * u.y;
	const float t1 = r * cos( phi );
	float t2 = r * sin( phi );
	const float s = 0.5 * ( 1.0 + Vh.z );
	t2 = ( 1.0 - s ) * sqrt( 1.0 - t1 * t1 ) + s * t2;
	const vec3 Nh = t1 * T1 + t2 * T2 + sqrt( max( 0.0, 1.0 - t1 * t1 - t2 * t2 ) ) * Vh;
	return normalize( vec3( alpha * Nh.x, alpha * Nh.y, max( 0.0, Nh.z ) ) );
}

/// THE RADIANCE AT A HIT — THE CARD FIRST, THE VOXELS WHERE NO CARD ANSWERS
/// (PHOTON-CARDS-2, SC-1d; F1-HITRES).
///
/// THE CARD. The surface cache holds, for every carded instance near the
/// camera, the lit radiance of its surface at the atlas's texel pitch — a
/// 2 m panel on a 128 page is 1.6 cm a texel, where the finest voxel a mirror
/// can read is two 7.8 cm cells (15.6 cm) wide. That width was the mirror band's
/// banding: every hit shaded from a 15.6 cm cell. So a hit asks the card of its
/// own instance first (`jahCardRadiance`, jah_rq_card.glsl: the instance by the
/// TLAS's instanceCustomIndex = the item slot, the pick, the through-the-wall
/// rule, the texel) and takes the card's radiance whole — weight 1, no
/// crossfade: a card texel is a surface's own answer, not a filtered volume.
/// The facing direction handed to the pick is the hit triangle's GEOMETRIC
/// normal (jah_rq_geom.glsl), so a hit near a box's edge reads its own face's
/// card. The read is texel-exact (the atlas has no mips until SC-2), so it is
/// taken only while the sample's FOOTPRINT is at most k card texels (Types.h
/// kCardFootprintTexels, measured): a wider lobe's one sample per frame on one
/// texel is speckle in a young view where the voxels' read is prefiltered at the
/// footprint's own mip.
///
/// THE VOXELS answer where no card does: an instance with no cards, a point no
/// card of it describes, a scene without a cache — and a card whose indirect
/// half has not been marched yet (it would read a black bounce; the header
/// there says why that happens). Their read, unchanged:
///
/// The anisotropic slots and not slot 0, because the spike measured exactly
/// what slot 0 costs: it is the voxel's radiance averaged over every direction,
/// so a panel lit on one face reads near-black from the lit side. Slots 1..3
/// are VctLighting's per-axis chains, each holding the two signs of one axis in
/// the two halves of its X range, and the read is upstream's own — the cone
/// tracer's, verbatim: pick the half by the sign of the direction's component,
/// weight the three axes by that component SQUARED (Vct_piece_ps.any:202-232).
/// The direction is the RAY's, not the hit surface's normal: the anisotropic
/// chains are indexed by the direction light is travelling THROUGH the cell,
/// and the quantity a reflection wants is the radiance leaving the hit towards
/// the ray's origin, which is the ray's own direction reversed twice — i.e. the
/// ray's direction. (The hit NORMAL is not available at all without fetching
/// the hit triangle's vertices, which needs a per-instance geometry table; it
/// is also not what upstream indexes by.)
///
/// THE VOXEL BIAS, stated because a suite asserts against it: a voxel stores
/// radiance PRE-MULTIPLIED by how much of the cell the surface covers, so a
/// point sample on a surface reads the surface's radiance times its coverage,
/// and trilinear filtering at the surface blends in the empty cell next door.
/// Dividing by the sampled opacity undoes exactly that, which is why the
/// suite's bar is a fraction of the panel's own lit albedo rather than equality:
/// a surface thinner than a cell, or at a grazing angle to the grid, keeps a
/// residual of its neighbours. The sample point is pushed HALF A CELL along the
/// ray, into the cell the surface occupies rather than onto its face.
///
/// THREE THINGS THIS FUNCTION DOES THAT UPSTREAM'S FIRST CUT DID NOT (DRAG-1):
///
///  1. THE RADIANCE IS IN THE SCENE'S UNITS, NOT THE BAKE'S. `VctLighting`
///     normalises everything it injects by the brightest light's radiance over
///     pi (its `autoMultiplier`), and the PIXEL path multiplies that back out
///     through `probeParams.multiplier` (Vct_piece_ps.any's `finalMultiplier`).
///     This function read the voxel raw, so a traced reflection was in
///     baking-normalised units and came out right ONLY when the brightest light
///     in the scene happened to have radiance pi — which is exactly a sun at
///     intensity 1 (powerScale = I*pi), i.e. every fixture the suites use, and
///     nothing else. A brighter lamp darkened every traced reflection by its
///     own factor. The engine now exports each cascade's `finalMultiplier` in
///     `voxelOrigin[c].w` (a slot that held the cascade's largest extent, which
///     nothing read).
///
///  2. THE CASCADE CHOICE IS BLENDED AT THE BOX FACE, not switched. Choosing the
///     first cascade that CONTAINS the hit steps the texel size 2x at cascade
///     0's face and 3x at cascade 1's, and a mirror looking across a hall shows
///     that step as a ring — the boundary of a box, drawn on a reflection. The
///     contribution now fades out over the last few texels of a cascade and the
///     next one out takes up the remainder, so the texel size changes over a
///     band instead of at a surface.
///
///  3. THE MIP IS CHOSEN FOR THE LOBE. `textureLod(..., 0.0)` made every ray
///     read the finest directional mip, so a rough reflection paid for the
///     anisotropic chain and never read it, and its variance was removed by the
///     spatial filter instead. The lobe has a width; at distance t it covers a
///     footprint, and a mip exists for exactly that. What is NOT taken is the
///     WHOLE lobe footprint: the temporal mean already averages up to N samples
///     of the lobe (N = 1/knobs2.w, the mean's memory floor), so pre-filtering
///     the whole lobe on top of that would blur it twice. The honest footprint
///     for ONE stochastic sample is the spacing BETWEEN the samples the mean
///     holds, which for N samples spread over the lobe goes as 1/sqrt(N). A
///     MIRROR takes LOD 0 and no blend widening at all — and LOD 0 is still two
///     cells, so this does not sharpen a mirror; only hit lighting can.
///
/// `lobeAlpha` is the GGX alpha of the surface the ray left and `t` the distance
/// it travelled.
vec3 hitRadiance( uint slot, vec3 hitPos, vec3 hitNormal, vec3 dir, float lobeAlpha, float t,
				  out bool ok )
{
	const float meanSamples0 = 1.0 / max( p.knobs2.w, 1e-3 );
	// THE SAMPLE'S FOOTPRINT AT THE HIT (the spacing between the samples the mean
	// holds; below): the card is read only while it is at most k card texels
	// (jahCardRadiance's gate) — a wider lobe reads the prefiltered voxels.
	{
		const float cardFootprint = lobeAlpha <= 0.01
										? 0.0
										: 2.0 * t * lobeAlpha / sqrt( max( meanSamples0, 1.0 ) );
		const vec3 card = jahCardRadiance( slot, hitPos, hitNormal, cardFootprint, ok );
		if( ok )
			return card;
	}
	// THE MIP IS CHOSEN FOR THE LOBE, and this wrapper is where that choice is
	// made — the shared function takes a FOOTPRINT in world units and knows
	// nothing about specular lobes. `textureLod(..., 0.0)` made every ray read
	// the finest directional mip, so a rough reflection paid for the
	// anisotropic chain and never read it. What is NOT taken is the WHOLE lobe
	// footprint: the temporal mean already averages up to N samples of the lobe
	// (N = 1/knobs2.w, the mean's memory floor), so pre-filtering the whole lobe
	// on top of that would blur it twice. The honest footprint for ONE
	// stochastic sample is the spacing BETWEEN the samples the mean holds, which
	// for N samples spread over the lobe goes as 1/sqrt(N).
	//
	// A MIRROR takes LOD 0, the containing cascade WHOLE and no crossfade at
	// all: a crossfade of two texel sizes is still a blur and a mirror is the
	// one surface that must not be blurred. (LOD 0 is still two cells, so this
	// does not sharpen a mirror; only hit lighting can.)
	const float kMirrorAlphaHere = 0.01;
	const bool mirror = lobeAlpha <= kMirrorAlphaHere;
	const float meanSamples = 1.0 / max( p.knobs2.w, 1e-3 );
	const float footprint = 2.0 * t * lobeAlpha / sqrt( max( meanSamples, 1.0 ) );
	return jahVoxelRadiance( hitPos, dir, footprint, mirror, ok );
}

/// An escaping sample, read at the SAME honest footprint as a hit (GA-SKY): the
/// spacing between the samples the temporal mean holds, as a half-angle —
/// lobeAlpha / sqrt(N) — and a mirror (lobeAlpha at or below the mirror cut)
/// reads the environment's finest mip.
vec3 skyRadiance( vec3 dir, float lobeAlpha )
{
	const float kMirrorAlphaHere = 0.01;
	const float meanSamples = 1.0 / max( p.knobs2.w, 1e-3 );
	const float tanHalf = lobeAlpha <= kMirrorAlphaHere ? 0.0
														: lobeAlpha / sqrt( max( meanSamples, 1.0 ) );
	return jahSkyRadiance( dir, tanHalf );
}

/// THE TEMPORAL MEAN'S PREVIOUS VALUE, reprojected and VALIDATED. The history
/// is a screen-space buffer, so last frame's texel at this pixel belongs to
/// whatever the camera was looking at then: the world position is projected
/// through the PREVIOUS frame's camera basis (the same five numbers, inverted)
/// to find where it was, and the distance stored beside it says whether the
/// surface found there is the same one. Without that test a disocclusion drags the previous surface's
/// reflection across the new one for as long as the mean remembers.
/// Returns rgb = the mean, w = how many frames are in it (0 = nothing usable).
///
/// `warmHeld` (DRAG-1, REFLECT F2 item 3) comes back 1.0 when a history texel
/// WAS found at the reprojected pixel and had already passed the filter's
/// warm-up (kWarmupFrames samples), but the distance test rejected it: the
/// surface here MOVED — and, in a view whose own history is warm, whenever
/// there is simply no texel to reproject from (off the previous picture,
/// behind the previous camera, a texel that never held an answer). Those are
/// the cases where the renderer's confidence in showing a ray at all must not
/// fall — see the count it seeds in main().
/// WHERE THIS SURFACE WAS IN THE LAST FRAME'S PICTURE — of THIS EYE (lane
/// REFLECT-VR-1). The reprojection runs entirely in the eye's own image and the
/// result is mapped back onto the eye's own HALF of the history: an eye's
/// temporal mean may never be read from the other eye's texels, whose radiance
/// belongs to a different camera and whose distances belong to different
/// silhouettes.
vec4 historyAt( EyeImage before, int eye, vec3 world, ivec2 traceRes, out float warmHeld,
				out float wasHitT )
{
	warmHeld = 0.0;
	wasHitT = 0.0;
	// A SURFACE THE CAMERA HAS JUST TURNED ONTO (PAN-SMEAR-1). It was behind the
	// previous camera or outside its picture, so there is no texel to reproject
	// from — and that used to read as "no history at all", the fresh-view case:
	// the count restarted at one and the filter faded the ray in from the probe
	// over kWarmupFrames. On a steady pan every column the turn reveals spent
	// eight frames in that fade, so the leading side of a glossy floor carried
	// a band eight columns wide showing the probe's answer instead of the
	// ray's — measured at 21 codes against the settled frame on a silver
	// floor at 1.5 degrees a frame, and zero with the rays off.
	//
	// It is the same case as the distance rejection below, and gets the same
	// answer: this frame's ray is the honest sample for a surface that is new
	// to the screen, and the renderer's confidence in showing a ray at all must
	// not fall because the CAMERA moved. What the warm-up protects is a view
	// that is itself young (a graded screenshot's lives about two frames) —
	// that is a fact about the VIEW, so it is read from the view's age and not
	// inferred from a missing texel.
	const float viewIsWarm = p.stereo.y >= kWarmupFrames ? 1.0 : 0.0;
	const vec3 d = world - before.camPos;
	const float z = dot( d, before.fwd );
	if( z <= 0.0 )
	{
		warmHeld = viewIsWarm;			// behind the camera it was
		return vec4( 0.0 );
	}
	const vec3 plane = before.perspective < 0.5 ? d - before.fwd * z : d / z;
	const vec3 q = plane - before.rayTL;
	const vec2 uv = vec2( dot( q, before.rayRight ) / dot( before.rayRight, before.rayRight ),
						  dot( q, before.rayDown ) / dot( before.rayDown, before.rayDown ) );
	if( any( lessThan( uv, vec2( 0.0 ) ) ) || any( greaterThanEqual( uv, vec2( 1.0 ) ) ) )
	{
		warmHeld = viewIsWarm;			// outside the previous picture it was
		return vec4( 0.0 );
	}
	const ivec2 pc = clamp( ivec2( eyeGlobalUv( uv, eye ) * vec2( traceRes ) ),
							ivec2( 0 ), traceRes - 1 );
	const vec2 wasPair = imageLoad( historyDistPrev, pc ).xy;
	const float wasDist = wasPair.x;
	const float dist = z;   // what THIS surface's distance was last frame
	// 5 % of the distance: far enough not to reject a surface the camera merely
	// moved along, tight enough that a silhouette's far side is a different
	// surface. A first frame stores 0 and is rejected here by construction.
	if( wasDist <= 0.0 || abs( wasDist - dist ) > 0.05 * max( dist, 1.0 ) )
	{
		// THE RADIANCE IS REJECTED; THE CONFIDENCE IS NOT (DRAG-1). A rejection
		// here means the surface at this pixel is not the one that was here
		// last frame — a drag, a camera move, a disocclusion — and this frame's
		// ray is the honest answer for the new one. What must NOT happen is the
		// filter's warm-up gate falling back to an eighth and fading the ray
		// out to the probe and in again over eight frames, once per rejection,
		// for every frame of a drag: that is a visible pulse on the reflection,
		// and it is the third of the flicker's three mechanisms. So a texel
		// that HAD a warm history hands its warm-up on, and nothing else.
		if( wasDist > 0.0 )
		{
			const vec4 hj = imageLoad( historyPrev, pc );
			if( finite1( hj.w ) && hj.w >= kWarmupFrames )
				warmHeld = 1.0;
		}
		// ...AND A TEXEL THAT HELD NO ANSWER AT ALL (PAN-SMEAR-1): floor that a
		// sideways move has just brought out from behind a pillar reprojects
		// onto the PILLAR, which is too rough to trace and has never stored a
		// distance. That read as a fresh pixel and faded in over the warm-up,
		// once per revealed column — the stepped trail beside every occluder
		// while the camera slides (4.4 codes over the floor on the pillar
		// fixture). It is the case at the top of this function again: the view
		// is warm, the surface is merely new to this pixel.
		warmHeld = max( warmHeld, viewIsWarm );
		return vec4( 0.0 );
	}
	const vec4 h = imageLoad( historyPrev, pc );
	if( !finite3( h.xyz ) || !finite1( h.w ) || h.w <= 0.0 )
		return vec4( 0.0 );
	// HOW FAR AWAY the thing in this mean was (the second channel): what main()
	// compares this frame's ray against when the camera has moved.
	wasHitT = finite1( wasPair.y ) ? max( wasPair.y, 0.0 ) : 0.0;
	return vec4( clamp( h.xyz, vec3( 0.0 ), vec3( kMaxRadiance ) ), h.w );
}

void main()
{
	const ivec2 traceRes = ivec2( p.resolution.xy );
	const ivec2 tc = ivec2( gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy );
	if( any( greaterThanEqual( tc, traceRes ) ) )
		return;

	// The pixel this ray is traced FOR is the centre of its block at full
	// resolution, so a half-resolution trace is not offset by half a pixel.
	const ivec2 fullRes = ivec2( p.resolution.zw );
	const ivec2 scale = max( fullRes / max( traceRes, ivec2( 1 ) ), ivec2( 1 ) );
	const ivec2 blockOrigin = tc * scale;
	const vec2 uv = ( vec2( blockOrigin ) + 0.5 * vec2( scale ) ) / vec2( fullRes );
	// WHICH EYE THIS PIXEL BELONGS TO, and its coordinate in that eye's own
	// image (lane REFLECT-VR-1). Every TEXTURE fetch below uses `uv` — the
	// coordinate over the whole target, which already lands in the right eye's
	// half of the depth, normal and roughness buffers — and every RAY below is
	// built from `localUv` through that eye's basis. With one eye the two are
	// the same coordinate and nothing branches.
	const int eye = eyeOfColumn( tc.x, traceRes.x );
	const vec2 localUv = eyeLocalUv( uv, eye );
	const EyeImage thisEye = eyeNow( eye );

	const float rawDepth = texture( sceneDepth, uv ).x;
	// Cleared depth at EITHER extreme is "nothing was drawn here" — correct with
	// and without reverse Z, the same test the march makes.
	if( rawDepth <= 0.0 || rawDepth >= 1.0 )
	{
		imageStore( historyDistCur, tc, vec4( 0.0 ) );
		return;
	}
	const float roughGate = p.knobs.x;
	const float feather = p.projParams.w;
	// WHAT THE G-BUFFER'S .y ACTUALLY IS, and getting this wrong is a factor of
	// ten in the lobe (second reader, C1). It is the GGX ALPHA — HlmsPbs runs
	// with `mPerceptualRoughness` true, so `pixelData.roughness` in the piece
	// that writes this channel is the perceptual value SQUARED (CLAUDE.md) —
	// packed by ogre-patch 0043 over [0.001, 1] and NOT over upstream's old
	// [0.02, 1]. Decoding it with the old range and then squaring it again ran
	// the lobe at perceptual to the FOURTH power: a surface authored at 0.3 was
	// traced as though it were 0.095, about ten times too sharp.
	//
	// The gate and the feather below are compared in PERCEPTUAL roughness,
	// because that is the number the World row shows and the owner states the
	// dial in. alpha 0.4 would be perceptual 0.63 — a different surface.
	const float alpha = max( texture( gBufShadowRoughness, uv ).y * 0.999 + 0.001, 1e-6 );
	const float roughness = sqrt( alpha );
	// TRACED UP TO THE FEATHER'S FAR EDGE, not to the cutoff: the band above the
	// cutoff is exactly where the two answers are being crossfaded, and a pixel
	// in it with no ray would make the crossfade a step again.
	if( roughness > roughGate + feather )
	{
		imageStore( historyDistCur, tc, vec4( 0.0 ) );
		return;
	}
	vec3 normalVS = texture( gBufNormals, uv ).xyz * 2.0 - 1.0;
	if( dot( normalVS, normalVS ) < 1e-6 )
	{
		imageStore( historyDistCur, tc, vec4( 0.0 ) );
		return;
	}
	normalVS = normalize( normalVS );

	const float dist = viewDistance( rawDepth );
	const vec3 world = worldAt( thisEye, localUv, dist );
	// The G-buffer normal is in Ogre's RIGHT-HANDED view space (HlmsPbs writes
	// `pixelData.normal` straight out — JahSsrRayMarch_ps.glsl's own note, which
	// is why the march negates z to march in a left-handed one). viewAxis* are
	// that space's three axes expressed in world space, so this is the plain
	// rotation back and nothing here has to know the handedness at all.
	const vec3 worldN = normalize( p.viewAxisX.xyz * normalVS.x +
								   p.viewAxisY.xyz * normalVS.y +
								   p.viewAxisZ.xyz * normalVS.z );

	const vec3 V = thisEye.perspective < 0.5 ? -thisEye.fwd
												: normalize( thisEye.camPos - world );
	if( dot( worldN, V ) <= 0.0 )
	{
		imageStore( historyDistCur, tc, vec4( 0.0 ) );
		return;
	}

	// The lobe is the alpha decoded above — NOT squared again; see the note
	// there. Floored so a mirror's tangent frame arithmetic stays finite.
	const float lobeAlpha = max( alpha, 1e-4 );
	// A tangent frame around the surface normal (Duff et al., branchless).
	const float sg = worldN.z >= 0.0 ? 1.0 : -1.0;
	const float a0 = -1.0 / ( sg + worldN.z );
	const float b0 = worldN.x * worldN.y * a0;
	const vec3 T = vec3( 1.0 + sg * worldN.x * worldN.x * a0, sg * b0, -sg * worldN.x );
	const vec3 B = vec3( b0, sg + worldN.y * worldN.y * a0, -worldN.y );
	const vec3 Ve = vec3( dot( V, T ), dot( V, B ), dot( V, worldN ) );
	const vec3 Ht = sampleGgxVndf( Ve, lobeAlpha, sample2( tc, uint( p.knobs.z ) ) );
	const vec3 H = normalize( Ht.x * T + Ht.y * B + Ht.z * worldN );
	vec3 L = reflect( -V, H );
	if( dot( L, worldN ) <= 0.0 )
		L = reflect( -V, worldN );		// the lobe left the surface: take the mirror

	// THE REPROJECTED HISTORY IS READ BEFORE THE RAY IS TRACED, because a
	// sample this frame cannot shade must not THROW IT AWAY (third reader,
	// item 2). A ray that finds geometry the voxels do not reach is a failure
	// of the CACHE, not evidence about the surface: storing a zero distance
	// there makes the next frame's reprojection reject, which resets the count
	// to 1, discards thirty frames of integral, and — through the warm-up —
	// throttles the ray to an eighth for eight frames afterwards. A glossy lobe
	// grazing the voxel volume's edge would never converge at all.
	float warmHeld;
	float wasHitT;
	const vec4 prev = historyAt( eyeBefore( eye ), eye, world, traceRes, warmHeld, wasHitT );

	// THE RAY. The origin is lifted off its own surface along the NORMAL rather
	// than along the ray, so a grazing reflection cannot re-enter the surface it
	// started on; tMin carries the same bias again for the same reason.
	const float bias = p.knobs2.y;
	const float maxT = p.knobs.y;
	rayQueryEXT rq;
	// NEAR COPIES ONLY (0x0F, Types.h kRayMaskNearField): a reflection traces the
	// level the ray rule chose, never the far field's coarse copies (A5b §4).
	rayQueryInitializeEXT( rq, tlas, gl_RayFlagsOpaqueEXT, 0x0Fu, world + worldN * bias,
						   bias, L, maxT );
	while( rayQueryProceedEXT( rq ) ) {}

	vec3 traced;
	bool ok = false;
	// How far this frame's ray went: the hit's distance, or — for a ray that
	// left the scene — a length no hit can have.
	float hitT = maxT * 2.0;
	if( rayQueryGetIntersectionTypeEXT( rq, true ) ==
		gl_RayQueryCommittedIntersectionTriangleEXT )
	{
		const float t = rayQueryGetIntersectionTEXT( rq, true );
		hitT = t;
		const uint hitSlot = uint( rayQueryGetIntersectionInstanceCustomIndexEXT( rq, true ) );
		vec3 hitN;
		jahHitGeometricNormal( hitSlot, uint( rayQueryGetIntersectionGeometryIndexEXT( rq, true ) ),
							   uint( rayQueryGetIntersectionPrimitiveIndexEXT( rq, true ) ),
							   rayQueryGetIntersectionObjectToWorldEXT( rq, true ), L, hitN );
		traced = hitRadiance( hitSlot, world + worldN * bias + L * t, hitN, L, lobeAlpha, t, ok );
		// A HIT THE CACHE CANNOT SHADE IS NOT A MISS. The ray found geometry;
		// the voxels simply do not reach it (outside every cascade, or a surface
		// too thin for the grid). Returning the sky there would draw a hole in
		// the middle of an object, which is worse than the probe's photograph —
		// so the pixel is handed BACK to the probe/sky answer already in the
		// texture, by writing nothing at all.
		if( !ok )
		{
			// A HIT THE CACHE CANNOT SHADE IS NOT EVIDENCE ABOUT THIS PIXEL.
			// The history is carried through UNCHANGED — same radiance, same
			// count, and a live distance so the next frame's reprojection still
			// finds it. A texel that has never had an answer (prev.w == 0)
			// writes a zero distance and the filter skips it this frame, which
			// is the honest "hand it back to the probe".
			imageStore( historyCur, tc, prev );
			imageStore( historyDistCur, tc, vec4( prev.w > 0.0 ? dist : 0.0, wasHitT, 0.0, 0.0 ) );
			return;
		}
	}
	else
	{
		traced = skyRadiance( L, lobeAlpha );		// route C: the escape
		ok = true;
	}
	if( !finite3( traced ) )
	{
		// A HIT THE CACHE CANNOT SHADE IS NOT EVIDENCE ABOUT THIS PIXEL.
		// The history is carried through UNCHANGED — same radiance, same
		// count, and a live distance so the next frame's reprojection still
		// finds it. A texel that has never had an answer (prev.w == 0)
		// writes a zero distance and the filter skips it this frame, which
		// is the honest "hand it back to the probe".
		imageStore( historyCur, tc, prev );
		imageStore( historyDistCur, tc, vec4( prev.w > 0.0 ? dist : 0.0, wasHitT, 0.0, 0.0 ) );
		return;
	}
	traced = clamp( traced, vec3( 0.0 ), vec3( kMaxRadiance ) );

	// THE TEMPORAL MEAN. 1/n while the mean is young — a true running average,
	// which is what converges fastest — with a floor so it keeps following a
	// scene that changes. A mirror (alpha at the clamp) converges on the first
	// frame anyway: every sample is the same ray.
	const float n = min( prev.w, 1.0 / max( p.knobs2.w, 1e-3 ) );
	float blend = prev.w > 0.0 ? max( 1.0 / ( n + 1.0 ), p.knobs2.w ) : 1.0;
	// THE MEAN IS FOR VARIANCE, AND A MIRROR HAS NONE (second reader, H1). The
	// floor on 1/n buys a still picture on a glossy surface by remembering
	// about thirty frames — and on a MIRROR there is nothing to remember: every
	// sample is the same ray, the estimator is exact on the first frame, and all
	// thirty frames of memory can do is carry the reflection of where the camera
	// USED to be. The reprojection moves the history to the right pixel; it
	// cannot fix the fact that a mirror's reflected direction changes with the
	// VIEW, so the old radiance is wrong at the new pose however well it is
	// reprojected. That is a visible smear behind any camera motion.
	//
	// So the memory is bought with the lobe's width and not given away: at
	// alpha 0 the blend is 1 (take this frame's answer whole), and it falls to
	// the floor over kMirrorAlpha — the alpha at which one sample stops being
	// the whole integral. 0.01 is perceptual 0.1, the roughness at which the
	// reflected lobe's half-width (~2*alpha) first exceeds a pixel of a 1080p
	// frame at a typical field of view.
	const float kMirrorAlpha = 0.01;
	// FROM THE PACKING FLOOR, not from zero (third reader, item 3). The
	// G-buffer cannot hold an alpha below 0.001 — ogre-patch 0043 packs over
	// [0.001, 1] — so 0.001 IS a mirror, and a ramp starting at 0 gives the
	// sharpest surface this renderer can express a blend of 0.972 rather than
	// 1: a mirror in a two-frame view would show 2.8 % probe, and a
	// perceptual-0.08 surface 30 % of it on its first frame.
	blend = max( blend, 1.0 - smoothstep( 0.001, kMirrorAlpha, lobeAlpha ) );
	// THE SURFACE IS THE SAME; WHAT IT REFLECTS IS NOT (PAN-SMEAR-1). The
	// reprojection above validates the SURFACE — same floor, same distance — and
	// under a camera that TRANSLATES that is not enough for any lobe, only
	// fastest to show on a sharp one: the floor beside a pillar's reflection was
	// reflecting the pillar a frame ago and reflects the sky now, its history
	// passes the distance test, and thirty frames of pillar drain out of the
	// mean one frame at a time — a stepped coloured trail behind every
	// reflection while the camera slides (3.4 codes over the floor on the pillar
	// fixture, after the two warm-up cases above were closed).
	//
	// The evidence that the reflected thing changed is in the ray itself: it
	// went a different DISTANCE than the rays in the mean did. So the mean keeps
	// the mean of that distance beside the surface's, and a moved camera whose
	// ray disagrees with it by more than the lobe could explain gives the memory
	// up — whole on a sharp lobe, most of it on a wide one, whose single sample
	// is noise and whose ghost is already a blur. ONLY WHEN THE CAMERA MOVED:
	// under a still camera the rays at a reflection's edge land on both sides of
	// it frame by frame, and that disagreement is the antialiased edge
	// converging, not evidence of anything. (A pure turn moves no reflection on
	// its surface — the eye has not moved — and the test is on position.)
	//
	// "MOVED" IS PARALLAX, NOT METRES. What changes a reflection is the angle the
	// eye's step subtends at the thing reflected, and the threshold is the angle
	// of half a trace pixel: below it the reflected image has not moved by
	// anything this buffer can show. An absolute step would be permanently
	// exceeded by a tracked VR head (a fraction of a millimetre of jitter every
	// frame), which would leave every reflected silhouette single-sample for
	// ever; at half a millimetre over two metres the parallax is a third of this
	// threshold, and a 0.25 m slide is ten times over it. An ORTHOGRAPHIC view
	// has one view direction for every pixel — a slide moves no reflection on
	// its surface — so the rule does not apply to it at all.
	float hitMean = hitT;
	if( prev.w > 0.0 && wasHitT > 0.0 )
	{
		const vec3 stepped = thisEye.camPos - eyeBefore( eye ).camPos;
		const float nearer = max( min( hitT, wasHitT ), 1e-3 );
		const float apart  = abs( hitT - wasHitT ) / nearer;
		const float wide   = smoothstep( kMirrorAlpha, 0.25, lobeAlpha );
		const float eyePixels = float( p.stereo.x > 0.5 ? traceRes.x / 2 : traceRes.x );
		const float halfPixelAngle = 0.5 * length( thisEye.rayRight ) / max( eyePixels, 1.0 );
		const bool moved = thisEye.perspective > 0.5 && length( stepped ) > nearer * halfPixelAngle;
		if( moved && apart > mix( 0.25, 4.0, wide ) )
			blend = max( blend, mix( 1.0, 0.35, wide ) );
		hitMean = mix( wasHitT, hitT, blend );
	}
	const vec3 mean = mix( prev.xyz, traced, blend );
	// THE COUNT, AND THE ONE PLACE IT IS SEEDED (DRAG-1). Ordinarily it is
	// exactly "how many samples are in this mean", which is what the blend
	// above and the filter's warm-up both read. On a MOTION-CAUSED rejection
	// (`warmHeld`) the mean restarts from this frame's sample — `blend` is 1,
	// `prev` is zero — but the pixel is seeded at the warm-up's own threshold
	// rather than at 1, so the filter composites the ray at full confidence
	// from the first frame instead of fading it out and back.
	//
	// It costs a little adaptation speed at that pixel: the running mean
	// resumes at 1/(kWarmupFrames+1) instead of 1/2, so it reaches the floor
	// (1/30) after about twenty frames rather than thirty. It does NOT cost
	// correctness: the sample it starts from is this frame's own ray.
	//
	// A pixel of a YOUNG VIEW is untouched — `warmHeld` is 0 until the view's
	// own history is kWarmupFrames old (`historyAt`) — so a freshly created view
	// (a graded screenshot's, which lives about two frames) still fades in
	// exactly as before, which is the guarantee the warm-up exists for.
	const float seed = max( prev.w, warmHeld * kWarmupFrames );
	const float count = min( seed + 1.0, 4096.0 );
	imageStore( historyCur, tc, vec4( mean, count ) );
	imageStore( historyDistCur, tc, vec4( dist, hitMean, 0.0, 0.0 ) );

	// THE COMPOSITE IS THE FILTER PASS' (round C). This shader now ends at the
	// temporal mean: `rq_reflect_filter.comp` reads it back through a
	// lobe-guided bilateral kernel and writes `jahSsrReflection`. Splitting
	// them is what makes a SPATIAL filter possible at all — a pixel cannot
	// blur with neighbours its own dispatch has not finished writing.
}
